{"author":{"id":"40e3ba2788b3b9fc163c354afc44e5f9d225bcf0a27e679aaa878dd6135c6dcb"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793914517,"nonce":"uORUYEe0lkyUrB5cmvgYRVuidMQ=","message":"Published November 5, 2026 from `bc33446f299e34257b2e029f9b02d5874ad1efaf`.\n\n- [PyPI package](https://pypi.org/project/uc2/3.0.0a4/)\n- [Native CLI downloads and matching source](https://github.com/evvaletov/uc2/releases/tag/v3.0.0-alpha.4)\n- [Distribution build and tests](https://github.com/evvaletov/uc2/actions/runs/42112187364)\n- [Native builds, integration tests, and fuzzing](https://github.com/evvaletov/uc2/actions/runs/42112187376)\n\n\nThe 35 Python tests pass on each of 20 installed wheels: CPython 3.10-3.14 on\nLinux x86-64, Windows x86-64, macOS Intel, and macOS Apple Silicon. They cover\nHuffman and rANS, existing reference archives, versions, literal semicolon\nnames, Unicode archive paths, corrupt inputs, resource limits, unsafe extraction\npaths, callback errors, garbage collection, and preservation of existing output.\nBinary CLI tests include every byte value, CR/LF, and Ctrl-Z.\n\nCI builds the source distribution, builds a wheel from that source, installs\nit, and passes the same 35 tests. Twine's strict metadata check passes for the\nsource archive and all wheels. The 21 files published on PyPI match the tested\nartifacts' SHA-256 hashes.\n\nA fresh virtual environment installed `uc2==3.0.0a4` directly from PyPI and\npassed all 35 tests. The installed `uc2` entry point reports `3.0.0a4`. All 27\npublished GitHub assets match the local release files' SHA-256 hashes.\n\nA separate Linux ASan/UBSan build passes all 35 tests. AddressSanitizer runs\nwith leak detection disabled for the Python process; this is not a leak-audit\nclaim. Core CI also passes native Linux/macOS/MSVC tests, a DJGPP cross-build,\nlibarchive and actual bsdtar tests, the WCX DLL ABI test on Windows, regression\nreplay, and 90-second decoder and round-trip fuzzing sessions. Native Total\nCommander UI and 32-bit WCX validation remain pending.\n\nEach standalone CLI is extracted from a tested wheel, smoke-tested on its\ntarget OS, and packaged with licenses and a binary hash. Release verification\ncompares that binary with its source wheel. The release supplies corresponding\nsource, `BUILD-INFO.json`, and `SHA256SUMS`.\n\n\nThe release-scoped task-qa/task-at attempt used a frozen snapshot and requested\nindependent reviews from Claude, Gemini, and Grok. Gemini returned a substantive\nreview. Claude subscription access failed, Grok reached its ten-minute timeout\nwithout a response, and API fallbacks were unavailable. Only one external\nreview completed; unavailable reviewers are not counted as clean reviews.\n\nGemini's findings led to these repairs:\n\n- Negative C-wrapper limits now fail instead of wrapping into large unsigned\n  values. The test also covers mutation of public Python limit attributes.\n- Invalid name components and file/directory path collisions fail during\n  metadata indexing.\n- Output exceeding a declared size raises `BadArchiveError` consistently.\n- Python-enabled CMake builds retain the normal CLI installation rules.\n- Documentation states POSIX extraction permissions precisely and clarifies\n  the read-only API and the two Python-version requirements for tests.\n\nThe suggested GC use-after-free was not substantiated: an executing bound C\nmethod holds a strong reference to its receiver, so the reader cannot be an\nunreachable cycle during its callback. A regression test collects during\nsource callbacks and confirms that a later unreachable reader/source cycle is\nreclaimed. No speculative GC guard was added.\n\nAdditional CI findings were repaired before publication: a Python 3.10 macro\ncould not be used in a preprocessor expression; Windows binary pipes used text\nmode; the libarchive download paired an xz hash with a gzip URL; and the DOS\nsignature check rejected version text from the current `file` utility.\n\nREADME prose scans found no em/en dashes, spaced double-hyphen clauses, curly\nquotes, stock formulaic phrases, or assistant-process leakage. CLI flags and\nordinary Markdown structure were retained. Prose edits were rescanned after\nrepair; this is a scoped scan, not a completed whole-project task-at audit.\nThe final scan covered 927 words in `README.md` and 578 in `python/README.md`,\nwith one post-edit rescan.\n\n\nThe project-wide audit is still open in git-bug `c9edf0b`. The unused standalone\nBLAKE3 module still fails official vectors and must not support integrity\nclaims. The reader uses legacy archive checksums. Turbo/method 80 and\nmulti-volume archives are unsupported; archive offsets are 32-bit. Python\ncreation remains a CLI operation. Extraction limits are documented in the\n[Python API guide](../../python/README.md).","files":null}]}